## Steps to reproduce
### Step 1. CubeMX config and code generation
1. Create the project from the board
a. Start CubeMX: run ~/STM32CubeMX/STM32CubeMX.
b. On the home screen, under "Start My project from ST Board", click ACCESS TO BOARD SELECTOR.
c. Type NUCLEO-F446RE in the "Commercial Part Number" search box and click the board in the list at the bottom.
d. Click Start Project (top right).
e. When asked "Initialize all peripherals with their default Mode?", click Yes.

Expected to see the chip drawing on the Pinout & Configuration tab, with the categories listed on the left.

2. Enable the 32 kHz crystal
a. Open System Core → RCC.
b. Set Low Speed Clock (LSE) to Crystal/Ceramic Resonator.
c. Leave High Speed Clock (HSE) as it is.

3. Configure the DAC
a. Open Analog → DAC.
b. Tick OUT1 Configuration. Do not tick OUT2 (that is PA5, the LED). Pin PA4 should turn green on the chip.
c. In the Parameter Settings tab below, set:
   - Output Buffer: Enable
   - Trigger: Timer 6 Trigger Out event
   - Wave generation mode: Disabled
d. Switch to the DMA Settings tab, click Add, and choose DAC1 in the new row.
f. Click that row and set, in the panel below it:
   - Mode: Circular
   - Data Width: Half Word for both Peripheral and Memory
   - Leave Direction as Memory To Peripheral and the stream as DMA1 Stream 5

4. Configure the timer
a. Open Timers → TIM6 and tick Activated.
b. In Parameter Settings, set:
   - Prescaler: 0
   - Counter Period: 10499
   - Trigger Event Selection: Update Event

5. Enable the button interrupt
a. Open System Core → NVIC.
b. Tick the Enabled box for EXTI line[15:10] interrupts.

6. Enable USART2 and check the board pins
a. Open Connectivity → USART2 and set Mode to Asynchronous. Leave Hardware Flow Control (RS232) on Disable.
b. In Parameter Settings, confirm Baud Rate 115200, Word Length 8 Bits, Parity None, Stop Bits 1.
c. Confirm in the pinout view:
   - PA2 and PA3 are green, labelled USART2_TX and USART2_RX.
   - PA4 is green, labelled DAC_OUT1.
   - PA5 is marked [LD1] and PC13 is marked [BUTTON]. Both are reserved by the board support code selected in the start-up window, so they do not appear as ordinary GPIO pins.

7. Check the clock
a. Click the Clock Configuration tab at the top.
b. Confirm that PLL Source Mux is on HSI and that APB1 Timer clocks (MHz) on the right shows 84.

If the APB1 timer clock shows a different number, change the Counter Period in step 4 to that clock in Hz divided by 8000, minus 1 (for example, 84,000,000 / 8000 - 1 = 10499).

8. Set up the project and generate
a. Click the Project Manager tab, then Project on the left.
b. Set:
   - Project Name: firmware
   - Project Location: <YOUR_PROJECT_DIR>/stm32-voice-reminder
   - Toolchain / IDE: CMake
c. Click Code Generator on the left and set:
   - Select "Copy only the necessary library files"
   - Tick "Generate peripheral initialization as a pair of '.c/.h' files per peripheral"
   - Leave "Keep User Code when re-generating" ticked
d. Click GENERATE CODE (top right) and close the dialog when it finishes. If CubeMX asks to download the STM32Cube FW_F4 firmware package, click Yes.

Expected result: a firmware/ folder in the repository containing firmware.ioc, CMakeLists.txt, Core/ and Drivers/.

### Step 2. The 1st Smoke test -- LED blink and button test (commit `3ffeeac`)
1. Connect the board
For bench work now, leave JP5 on U5V and plug the Nucleo into the PC by USB; the E5V arrangement is only tested in the last M1 step. The speaker does not need to be wired yet.

2. Build and flash
Needs arm-none-eabi-gcc, CMake, Ninja and OpenOCD installed on the PC.
a. Go to the firmware folder:
   cd <YOUR_PROJECT_DIR>/stm32-voice-reminder/firmware
b. Configure the build (only needed once, or after CMake files change):
   cmake --preset Debug
c. Build:
   cmake --build build/Debug
   The last lines show the memory use (about 12.3 KB of FLASH with the smoke test). The result is build/Debug/firmware.elf.
d. Flash, verify and restart the board:
   openocd -f board/st_nucleo_f4.cfg -c "program build/Debug/firmware.elf verify reset exit"
   Look for "** Programming Finished **" and "** Verified OK **" in the output. The "Unable to match requested speed" lines are harmless.

If the build fails, do not flash: OpenOCD would write the previous firmware.elf again.

Expected result with the smoke test in Core/Src/main.c: the green LD2 blinks about once per second; each press of the blue USER button switches between slow and fast blinking (about five times per second).

### Step 3. The 2nd Smoke test -- DAC tone and gain test (commit `d0a0d7e`)
Needs the following:
  1.Adafruit STEMMA Speaker – Plug and Play Audio Amplifier
  2.Adafruit STEMMA JST-PH 2mm 3-Pin to Female Socket Cable

a. With the USB still unplugged, connect the speaker with the 3-Pin to Female Socket Cable, and connect this cable with the board as following: The red wire is on 5V (CN7 pin 18), the black wire on GND, and the white wire on PA4 (CN7 pin 32). Check the three wires on the Morpho pins, then plug in the USB.
b. Start the serial monitor: stty -F /dev/ttyACM0 115200 raw -echo && cat /dev/ttyACM0
c. In `firmware/`, build and flash:
   - cmake --build build/Debug
   - openocd -f board/st_nucleo_f4.cfg -c "program build/Debug/firmware.elf verify reset exit"
d. Turn the speaker's trim pot to about the middle to start with.
e. Press the USER button to step through the gains (0.03 to 0.20).

Expected result:
- After flashing: silence, LD2 blinking once a second, and gain 0.00 in the serial terminal.
- Each USER button press: a steady 500 Hz tone getting louder: 0.03, 0.05, 0.08, 0.12, 0.20, then back to 0.00.

### Step 4. ADPCM speech playback with fades and button stop (commit `679a3ce`)
Uses the same wiring as step 3. Also needs FFmpeg, Python 3 and bc on the PC.

a. Record a short speech clip yourself, 2 to 5 seconds, for example a sentence like "Time to take your medicine". Any format FFmpeg reads works (WAV, MP3, M4A); WAV is best. Leave about half a second of silence before and after the words. Recordings are personal, so the audio/ folder is ignored by Git and you have to provide your own.
b. Put the recording in the audio/ folder, for example audio/audio_sample.mp3.
c. From the repository root, encode it to 8 kHz IMA-ADPCM:
   sh tools/encode_clip.sh audio/audio_sample.mp3 test_clip 1.2
   - test_clip is the output name; the firmware expects exactly this name.
   - 1.2 is optional: the number of seconds to cut from the start, here to remove a click at the beginning of the recording. A fourth number, also optional, sets where the clip ends, in seconds.
   The script prints the peak level and the clip length, and writes to audio/:
   - test_clip.adpcm: the encoded clip
   - test_clip_8k.wav: the encoded clip decoded again; listen to it on the PC to hear what the board will play
   - test_clip.c: the clip as a C array, compiled into the firmware
d. Run the PC-side tests from the repository root:
   cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja
   cmake --build build
   ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure
   All three tests (ima_adpcm, pcm_dac, player) must pass.
e. With the board connected, start the serial monitor: stty -F /dev/ttyACM0 115200 raw -echo && cat /dev/ttyACM0
f. In firmware/, configure, build and flash:
   - cmake --preset Debug
   - cmake --build build/Debug
   - openocd -f board/st_nucleo_f4.cfg -c "program build/Debug/firmware.elf verify reset exit"
   If configuring stops with "Missing .../audio/test_clip.c", step c has not been done.
g. Press the USER button to play the clip, and press it again to stop.

Expected result:
- After flashing: silence, LD2 blinking once a second, and "ready: press USER to play" in the serial terminal.
- First press: "play"; the clip plays and repeats after a 2-second pause until stopped.
- Next press: "stop"; the sound fades out quickly. Pressing again plays from the beginning.
- No clicks at the start or end of each repetition or on stop, and no crackle or dropouts during the speech.
- The speech sounds slightly worse than test_clip_8k.wav on the PC. This is expected, from the small speaker and the unfiltered DAC output, and is not a level problem: turning the trim pot down only makes it quieter.
